page ref_ws_lab {
  title = "wscript API: Lab and Segment"
  chapter_header "wscript API: Lab and Segment" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "12 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    This chapter is the method-by-method reference for the two handles a
    script starts from: `Lab`, which every script receives, and `Segment`,
    which a lab handle gives out by name. The [scripting guide](guide_scripting)
    explains where scripts run and how a provision reaches the machines it
    needs; the [networking guide](guide_networking) explains what the segment
    rules do on the wire. The machine handle is in
    [wscript API: Machine](ref_ws_machine), and the terminal handle, the
    record types and the module functions are in
    [wscript API: Term, types and the vmlab module](ref_ws_types).
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Every script begins with `use vmlab`. A provision script or an ad-hoc
    `vmlab script` run defines `fn main(lab: Lab)`; an event handler defines
    `fn handle(event: Event, lab: Lab)`. Both handles are opaque: they carry
    no fields, only methods. Every method that can fail returns a wscript
    `Result` whose error is a string, and an error that propagates out of
    `main` fails the provision run and therefore `vmlab up`. The signatures
    below use the parameter names the host registers; the generated
    `vmlab.wscripti` interface file spells them `a0`, `a1` and so on.
  DOC

  callout "Vm is an alias" { kind = :note body = <<DOC
    `Vm` is a silent alias for `Machine`, kept so first-boot scripts sealed
    into older templates keep compiling. New scripts write `Machine`.
  DOC
  }

  h2 "Lab.name"
  p "The lab's name, as the lab file declares it."
  code wscript { source = "fn name(self) -> string" }
  p <<DOC
    Returns the name from the `lab "<name>" {}` block. It never fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    lab.log("provisioning lab " + lab.name())
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.log"
  p "Write one line to the lab log and to the terminal of the CLI that started the run."
  code wscript { source = "fn log(self, msg: string)" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`msg`" | "string" | "The text to write. A newline is appended." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The line goes to `lab.log` under the lab's state directory (see
    [Files and directories](ref_paths)) and is streamed live to the `vmlab up`
    or `vmlab script` invocation that is running the script. From an event
    handler it lands in the lab log only. It never fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    for m in lab.machines() {
        lab.log(m.name() + ": " + m.state())
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.machine"
  p "The handle for one machine of either kind, by name."
  code wscript { source = "fn machine(self, name: string) -> Result[Machine, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The name of a `vm {}` or `container {}` block in the lab file." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns a [Machine](ref_ws_machine) handle running as the agent identity.
    The error names the machine when the lab declares none by that name. Use
    this when the script does not care which kind it is talking to; an event
    handler that reads `event.vm` usually does not.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn handle(event: Event, lab: Lab) {
    if event.name == "container.crashed" {
        let Ok(m) = lab.machine(event.vm) else { return }
        let started = m.start()
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.machines"
  p "Every machine in the lab, VMs and containers alike."
  code wscript { source = "fn machines(self) -> List[Machine]" }
  p <<DOC
    Returns one handle per declared machine. The order is the runtime's own
    and is not the declaration order. It never fails; an empty lab returns an
    empty list.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    for m in lab.machines() {
        let r = m.wait_ready(300)
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.vm"
  p "The handle for one VM, by name, refusing a container."
  code wscript { source = "fn vm(self, name: string) -> Result[Machine, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The name of a `vm {}` block." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The same handle `Lab.machine` returns, with a kind check in front. A name
    the lab does not declare fails as `Lab.machine` does. A name that is a
    container fails with a message saying so and pointing at `lab.container()`,
    which tells you more than "no such machine" would. Every operation on the
    returned handle is available on containers too; the check exists only so a
    script that knows what it declared reads well.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else {
        lab.log("no dc01")
        return
    }
    let r = dc.wait_ready(600)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.this_vm"
  p "The machine whose provision block declared the running script."
  code wscript { source = "fn this_vm(self) -> Result[Machine, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Set for a `provision {}` block declared inside a `vm {}` or
    `container {}`, and for a template's first-boot script, where it is the
    build VM. It fails with a message saying so from an event handler and from
    `vmlab script`, because neither has an owning machine.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    Inside a template first-boot script the handle it returns is gated: on
    that handle, `is_ready` and `wait_ready` mean "the agent answers" rather
    than full readiness, because full readiness is unreachable until the
    script itself returns. See [Machine.is_ready](ref_ws_machine).
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let vm = lab.this_vm().expect("no target vm")
    match vm.exec("hostname", []) {
        Ok(r) => lab.log("running on " + r.stdout),
        Err(e) => lab.log("exec failed: " + e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.vms"
  p "The VMs of the lab, and only the VMs."
  code wscript { source = "fn vms(self) -> List[Machine]" }
  p <<DOC
    `Lab.machines` filtered to machines whose kind is `vm`. It never fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    for vm in lab.vms() {
        let s = vm.snapshot("baseline")
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.container"
  p "The handle for one container, by name, refusing a VM."
  code wscript { source = "fn container(self, name: string) -> Result[Machine, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The name of a `container {}` block." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The mirror of `Lab.vm`: the same handle, with the opposite kind check. A
    VM's name fails with a message pointing at `lab.vm()`. See
    [lab containers](guide_containers) for what a container can and cannot do
    at call time.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(web) = lab.container("web") else { return }
    match web.logs(50) {
        Ok(text) => lab.log(text),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.containers"
  p "The containers of the lab, and only the containers."
  code wscript { source = "fn containers(self) -> List[Machine]" }
  p <<DOC
    `Lab.machines` filtered to machines whose kind is `container`. It never
    fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    for c in lab.containers() {
        if !c.is_healthy() { lab.log(c.name() + " is unhealthy") }
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Lab.segment"
  p "The handle for one segment, by name."
  code wscript { source = "fn segment(self, name: string) -> Result[Segment, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The name of a `segment {}` block in the lab file." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The error names the segment and the lab when no segment of that name is
    assembled. A handle is a name, not a lock: every method on it looks the
    segment up again, so a segment torn down after the handle was taken fails
    on the next call with "segment is gone".
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let id = lan.dns_set("intranet.corp.local", "10.0.0.10")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.name"
  p "The segment's name."
  code wscript { source = "fn name(self) -> string" }
  p "Returns the name the handle was taken with. It never fails."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(seg) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    lab.log("rules on " + seg.name())
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.dns_set"
  p "Add a static DNS record, or a wildcard, to the segment's zone."
  code wscript { source = "fn dns_set(self, name: string, ip: string) -> Result[int, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "An exact name, or a `*.`-prefixed wildcard pattern. Taken verbatim; the segment's DNS suffix is not appended." |
      | "`ip`" | "string" | "The IPv4 address to answer with." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns a rule id for `Segment.dns_clear`. An exact name becomes a static
    record that replaces any earlier record for that name; a pattern beginning
    with `*.` becomes a wildcard. Lookup precedence in the zone is sinkhole,
    then exact record, then wildcard, then the upstream forwarder, else
    NXDOMAIN.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It fails when `ip` does not parse as an IPv4 address, when the segment is
    gone, or when the segment has DNS disabled.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    match lan.dns_set("*.apps.corp.local", "10.0.0.20") {
        Ok(id) => lab.log("wildcard rule " + id),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.dns_sinkhole"
  p "Answer NXDOMAIN for every name matching a pattern."
  code wscript { source = "fn dns_sinkhole(self, pattern: string) -> Result[int, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`pattern`" | "string" | "The name or `*.`-wildcard to sink." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns a rule id for `Segment.dns_clear`. Sinkholes win over every other
    kind of record; among sinkholes the most specific pattern wins and
    declaration order breaks ties. The script surface always sinks with
    NXDOMAIN; the other sinkhole modes are available in the lab file only,
    see [lab and segment](ref_lab). It fails on a gone segment or one with
    DNS disabled.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let id = lan.dns_sinkhole("*.telemetry.example.com")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.dns_clear"
  p "Remove a rule added by `Segment.dns_set` or `Segment.dns_sinkhole`."
  code wscript { source = "fn dns_clear(self, rule_id: int) -> Result[bool, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`rule_id`" | "int" | "The id an earlier `dns_set` or `dns_sinkhole` returned." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns `true` when something was removed and `false` when the id matched
    nothing. Records the lab file declared have no id and cannot be cleared
    from a script. It fails on a gone segment or one with DNS disabled.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let Ok(id) = lan.dns_sinkhole("updates.example.com") else { return }
    let removed = lan.dns_clear(id)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.block"
  p "Drop every packet to a destination address or range."
  code wscript { source = "fn block(self, cidr: string) -> Result[int, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`cidr`" | "string" | "An IPv4 network in CIDR form, or a single IPv4 address." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns a rule id for `Segment.unblock`. The rule applies to every
    protocol and port. It fails when `cidr` is neither a network nor an
    address, when the segment is gone, or when the segment runs no network
    services, which is the case for a global segment the supervisor gateways.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    match lan.block("203.0.113.0/24") {
        Ok(id) => lab.log("block rule " + id),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.block_port"
  p "Drop packets to a destination for one protocol and port."
  code wscript { source = "fn block_port(self, cidr: string, proto: string, port: int) -> Result[int, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`cidr`" | "string" | "An IPv4 network in CIDR form, or a single address." |
      | "`proto`" | "string" | "`tcp`, `udp` or `icmp`." |
      | "`port`" | "int" | "The destination port, 0 to 65535." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns a rule id for `Segment.unblock`. It fails on a malformed `cidr`,
    an unknown `proto`, a port out of range, a gone segment, or a segment
    without network services.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let id = lan.block_port("0.0.0.0/0", "tcp", 25)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.unblock"
  p "Remove a block or redirect rule by id."
  code wscript { source = "fn unblock(self, rule_id: int) -> Result[bool, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`rule_id`" | "int" | "The id `block`, `block_port` or `redirect` returned." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns `true` when a rule was removed and `false` when nothing carried
    that id. Despite the name it removes redirects too; the block and
    redirect tables share one id space. It fails on a gone segment or one
    without network services.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let Ok(id) = lan.block("198.51.100.7") else { return }
    vmlab::sleep_ms(30000)
    let gone = lan.unblock(id)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.redirect"
  p "Rewrite the destination of packets from one address to another."
  code wscript { source = "fn redirect(self, from: string, to: string) -> Result[int, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`from`" | "string" | "The original destination, `ip` or `ip:port`." |
      | "`to`" | "string" | "The new destination, `ip` or `ip:port`." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns a rule id that `Segment.unblock` removes. Redirects are evaluated
    before blocks. The script surface leaves the protocol unset, so the rule
    matches every protocol; a protocol-specific redirect is declared in the
    lab file. It fails when either endpoint has a malformed IP or port, or on
    a gone segment or one without network services.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let id = lan.redirect("8.8.8.8:53", "10.0.0.1:53")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.forward"
  p "Publish a guest TCP port on the host."
  code wscript { source = "fn forward(self, host_port: int, vm: string, guest_port: int) -> Result[int, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`host_port`" | "int" | "The host port to listen on, bound on every host address." |
      | "`vm`" | "string" | "The machine whose port to reach, by name." |
      | "`guest_port`" | "int" | "The port inside that machine." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns the forward's id. The forward is TCP only and is resolved once: the
    machine's first agent-reported IPv4 address at the time of the call, so
    the machine must be up and leased. It fails when either port is out of
    range, when the machine does not exist, when the agent reports no IPv4
    address yet, when the segment has no NAT (a forward needs egress to
    originate the guest-side connection), or on a gone segment or one without
    network services. Forwards the lab file declares are computed up front
    instead; see [networking](guide_networking).
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let Ok(web) = lab.container("web") else { return }
    let r = web.wait_ready(120)
    let id = lan.forward(8080, "web", 80)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.route_to"
  p "Opt this segment into routing to another. Not available from scripts in this release."
  code wscript { source = "fn route_to(self, other: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`other`" | "string" | "The segment to route to." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The method compiles and always fails with "inter-segment routing is not
    yet available from scripts". Declare routing in the lab file instead; see
    [networking](guide_networking).
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    match lan.route_to("dmz") {
        Ok(_) => lab.log("routed"),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.unroute_to"
  p "Reverse `Segment.route_to`. Not available from scripts in this release."
  code wscript { source = "fn unroute_to(self, other: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`other`" | "string" | "The segment to stop routing to." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Always fails with the same message as `Segment.route_to`.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    let r = lan.unroute_to("dmz")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Segment.rules"
  p "The segment's live block and redirect rules, as JSON text."
  code wscript { source = "fn rules(self) -> Result[string, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Returns a JSON array in evaluation order: redirects first, then blocks,
    each in insertion order. Every element carries `id`, `kind` (`redirect`
    or `block`) and a one-line `description`. A block also carries `cidr`,
    and `port` when it has one; a redirect carries `from` and `to`; either
    carries `proto` when it is protocol-specific. Rules from the lab file and
    rules added by script appear together. DNS rules are not included. It
    fails on a gone segment or one without network services.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(lan) = lab.segment("lan") else { return }
    match lan.rules() {
        Ok(json) => lab.log(json),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }
}
